# ByteBites Class Diagram

Derived from [bytebites_spec.md](../bytebites_spec.md). Each property/method below is traced to a specific line in the spec.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class Customer {
        -customerId: String
        -name: String
        -email: String
        -password: String
        -transactions: List~Transaction~
        +addTransaction(transaction: Transaction) void
        +isVerifiedUser() boolean
        +getTransactions() List~Transaction~
    }

    class Item {
        -name: String
        -price: double
        -category: String
        -popularityRating: double
    }

    class Transaction {
        -transactionId: String
        -date: Date
        -selectedItems: Set~Item~
        +addItem(item: Item) void
        +removeItem(item: Item) void
        +computeTotalCost() double
    }

    class Menu {
        -items: List~Item~
        +addItem(item: Item) void
        +removeItem(item: Item) void
        +filterByCategory(category: String) List~Item~
    }

    Customer "1" o-- "many" Transaction : transactions
    Transaction "1" o-- "many" Item : selectedItems
    Menu "1" o-- "many" Item : catalog
```

## Design notes / traceability

| Spec requirement | Modeled as |
|---|---|
| "tracking their names and their past purchase history so the system can verify they are real users" | `Customer.name`, `Customer.transactions`, `Customer.isVerifiedUser()` |
| "track the name, price, category, and popularity rating for every item" | `Item` fields |
| "a digital list that holds all items and lets us filter by category" | `Menu` (added beyond your requested 4 classes — nothing else in the diagram can satisfy this requirement without it) |
| "group them into a single transaction... store the selected items and compute the total cost" | `Transaction.selectedItems`, `Transaction.computeTotalCost()` |
| "past purchase history" | `Customer.transactions` — a customer can have several `Transaction`s directly; no separate history class |

## Open questions (assumptions I made to fill gaps in the spec)

- **`isVerifiedUser()`**: spec says purchase history lets the system "verify they are real users" but doesn't define the check. I assumed non-empty `transactions`; could instead (or also) mean a verified `email`/`password`.
- **`email`/`password`**: added as plain fields per your instruction. No hashing/auth logic modeled — flag if this needs to go further than a data property.
- **`selectedItems` as a `Set`**: since it's a set of distinct items, a `Transaction` can't currently represent "2x Spicy Burger" — only presence/absence of each item. Confirm that's intended before this becomes code.
- **`popularityRating`**: now a fixed field with no update method, set at creation.
